ol  in the context of what that tool or item  was originally designed for it has a  fixed functionality as far as you're  concerned so if you have a hammer you're  thinking this is used for driving nails  into wood it's not useful for anything  else and that of course means you might  miss out on other possible uses for it  and this is perfectly demonstrated by a  famous problem called the candle problem  in the candle problem participants are  presented with a hammer a box of tack  and that's it and they're told they need  to attach the candle so they also  presented with a candle and they're told  there needs to be attached the candle to  the wall using the hammer and the box of  tacks and it needs to be out of burn and  stay attached and light up the room so  what they'll normally do is take take  the hammer take the tacks and try and  drive the tacks through the candle into  the wall so that the candle stays  attached to the wall and the lights up  the room  of course this just breaks the candle it  won't work and what they actually need  to do is to empty the box of tacks use  the box as a kind of shelf nail that to  the wall and then stand the candle in  the box so in order to come to that  conclusion they've thought of that box  of tacks or something other than just a  box of tacks they thought of it as a  shelf they've overcome their functional  fixedness and this is the same kind of  thing we see when Jackie Chan sees an  umbrella and doesn't just see an  umbrella he sees something that he can  fight with it's also the same kind of  thing that we use in puzzle solving mode  and play computer games so in a computer  game say portal you're presented with a  tool say the portal gun and shown away  you can use it and then the puzzle comes  from coming up with a new problem and  then having to use that tool you've been  taught in a new and inventive way so  you're using the same tool the  overcoming of functional fixedness to  come up with a new way to use it  the real aha moment is when you have  that shift in perception that allows you  to view everything that's happened in a  new light so how can we make ourselves  overcome this functional fixedness have  more shifts in perception be more  resourceful  well according to studies that use the  candle problem it seems that the more  stressed we are and the more motivated  we are the less successful we are at  overcoming these kinds of problems and  that's because when we're very stressed  and very full of adrenaline and dopamine  we tend to be focused on just one answer  we just try and repeat that one solution  until we get what we want  on the other hand when you relax more  you can explore the myriad different  ideas and different options explore your  connectome and come up with something  entirely new of course in the case of  Jackie Chan he's doing all this in a  split instant in order to grab something  in his environment and use it in a  creative way and this demonstrates the  importance of being able to stay calm  and cool in a crisis and you can do this  not only  things like CBT but also meditation and  by improving your cardiovascular fitness  in order to lower your heart rate so  that your whole physiology is karma even  when you're exerting yourself and so  actually instead of being stressed when  you're trying to figure out the solution  to a problem what you actually do is  just walk away and then come back to it  and that's probably why I'll come with  my solutions to programming problems  when I get up to go to the loop and  there's another reason for this and  that's because when you change your  environment that creates a perception  shift because all these new neurons are  firing and allows you to view what  you're doing in a different way so the  doorway effect describes why we often  forget what we went into a room for and  that's because you've gone into a new  environment new neurons are firing and  therefore you forget the reason you went  in there your perspective has changed so  this could be used in a positive way  when you're stuck on a problem if you  just take a break and expose yourself to  a new environment then you can change  your way of thinking just enough to get  a new perspective on the issue there are  also a bunch of different protocols you  can use to give yourself a new  perspective often a good option is just  to ask yourself different questions  which will reframe the problem so you  might ask yourself how would my friend  bla solve this problem or how would I  solve this problem ideally if I had all  the tools I needed and this helps you to  think about it in a new and different  way one of the best options is simply to  run through all of them raw materials at  your disposal so instead of thinking  I've got a hammer  I've got tacks I've got a candle you now  think I've got string in the wick I've  got wax I've got wood I've got metal  then you think of all the different  tools you've got hammer box etc and all  the different ways you can use them  bashing things prying nails out of walls  using it for leverage etc and this way  you see more options and you can come up  with more solutions changing your  perspective on things is something that  the brain loves to do because it gives  us more options and of course using  tools is one of the things that helped  our evolution to a massive extent the  first time I picked up a stick and we  used it to fish for ants in a tree that  was an example of us overcoming  functional fixedness and developing  useful tools one of the best ways to  change your perception to get a shift in  perspective  is to seek moments of or this is called  or cultivation so when you're standing  on top of a mountain or when you're  faced with some exotic creature or when  you had something challenge your whole  belief system like an episode of planet  Earth - then you sometimes feel  incredibly small and you get this  incredible sense of wonder and awe  neurochemically this is the result of a  release of all kinds of hormones and  neurotransmitters and it might be  because your brain is having to  completely rethink its stance on so many  different things  rebuild reassess lots of different  networks because you're having to  rethink your position in the world it's  changed the way you've seen all kinds of  different memories and ideas that you've  had before it's such a grand data dump  that it changes much of your connectome  and this massive restructuring triggers  a kind of amazing feeling of awe and  wonder of being small and have limitless  possibilities and if you seek these  moments and increase your exposure to  the novel and the new and the exciting  then you could be naturally far more  inventive and creative so thanks ton for  watching guys hope you found this video  useful and interesting if you did please  leave a like comment down below let me  know you think and of course I've got  many more like this on the way on brain  training fitness bodybuilding martial  arts you name it productivity so if that  sounds good then please subscribe hit  the bell button for notifications and  I'll see you next time thanks a ton for  watching bye for now

